---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const B = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') || '';
---
<Layout title="About — Interactive Atlas" description="How Interactive Atlas works: static pages, browser-side instruments, no accounts and no tracking.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="crumb"><a href={B + '/'}>atlas</a><span class="sep">/</span>about</p>
      <h1>Learning by touching</h1>
      <p>
        Interactive Atlas started as an interactive science reader and grew: physics, chemistry,
        biology and mathematics now share the shelf with history, geography, economics, computer
        science and astronomy. The plan is simple — every subject gets a ladder from foundational
        to expert, and every lesson on it must be something you can <em>manipulate</em>, not just
        read.
      </p>

      <h2>What a lesson is</h2>
      <p>
        Each lesson is a short page assembled from five reusable instruments: a plotter for
        equations, a slider lab for live models, a timeline for chronologies, a step-wise
        derivation for proofs, and a draggable vector diagram. The instruments are small
        hand-written JavaScript modules — no frameworks, no build-time magic in the browser.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy, precisely</h2>
      <p>
        There are no accounts, no cookies and no analytics. The entire site is static HTML, CSS
        and JavaScript; the only "database" is a read-only search index your browser downloads and
        queries locally. Nothing you type, drag or read is recorded anywhere.
      </p>

      <h2>Where content comes from</h2>
      <p>
        Lessons are drafted with AI assistance against standard references (NCERT, OpenStax,
        Wikipedia and primary sources), then reviewed by a human before publishing. The generation
        pipeline runs offline in the repository's tooling — never in your browser.
      </p>

      <h2>Colophon</h2>
      <p>
        Built with <a href="https://astro.build" rel="noopener">Astro</a>, Spectral and IBM Plex.
        Hosted on GitHub Pages. The <a href="https://github.com/adineo-cmd/interactive-science" rel="noopener">source is public</a> —
        pull requests and topic requests are welcome.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
